WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.499
Okay, let's get into audience questions for
the week and they are good and they are very

00:00:03.499 --> 00:00:05.280
needy this week.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.210
Hey David, when will the constitution be officially
outdated?

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.960
This is so loaded and it just, uh, to, to
begin with.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.289
There are lots of people who just say never
there called constitutional fetishists.

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:22.720
Sometimes they call into the show, but if
we want to have a sensible adult conversation,

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.040
the first question is, what do you mean by
outdated?

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:31.240
If you mean, does the constitution still effectively
establish the federal government and give

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.120
us, uh, the original set of powers for the
federal government?

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.120
It still does that.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.190
And in that sense, it's not outdated in that
it's still outlines the government that we

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.620
have today.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.130
If the question, which I think is a more practical
question becomes, does the constitution still

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:51.480
serve as a relevant document to answer questions
about how things should work today?

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:56.520
The answer is that with every passing year,
the constitution increasingly fails to be

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.719
a document that gives us those answers.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:02.710
Uh, so, so in many ways the constitution is
already outdated.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.049
Look at the chaos around gun laws.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:10.350
Is it the guns that were around at the time
of the constitutions writing or do we need

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:18.030
to interpret which guns and types of arms
fall under the jurisdiction of what the founders

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.599
meant when the Second Amendment was established?

00:01:20.599 --> 00:01:23.579
Well, what exists, it's immediate and total
chaos.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:30.270
The problem is either it's outdated or it
isn't, and the chaos starts when we start

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.509
arguing about which pieces are outdated and
in which way, and we start trying to suss

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:42.009
out the intentions of the founders versus
whether a totally literal interpretation should

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:43.009
be applied.

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:47.689
But if it is a little literal interpretation,
it should be applied based on the world that

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:52.849
existed at the time that the constitution
or its amendments were written, not necessarily

00:01:52.849 --> 00:01:58.669
a literal interpretation based on 2019 that
makes it very, very little sense and you end

00:01:58.669 --> 00:02:03.509
up just getting a kind of partisan strategy
around the constitution to argue one way when

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:07.549
it favors your side politically and argue
a different way when it doesn't favor your

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:08.569
side politically.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:10.719
How does the First Amendment apply to the
Internet?

00:02:10.719 --> 00:02:15.980
The answer is it actually depends on whether
a president has put conservative or liberal

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:21.519
justices on the supreme court doesn't really
seem like that is a particularly uptodate

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:23.909
document, if that's what it depends on.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:27.090
How does the fourth amendment apply to motor
vehicles?

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:32.170
The answer is, it depends on whether presidents
have recently been able to get conservative

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.969
or liberal justices on the supreme court.

00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:42.340
Thomas Jefferson, who believed that Americans
should revisit the constitution every 20 years

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.599
probably was onto something.

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:49.450
Although 20 years is probably too frequently,
uh, maybe it was based on life expectancy

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.430
at the time, I don't know.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:57.260
But the idea there is a good one and there's
an argument to be made, uh, which we often

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:03.200
hear from those who believe that the constitution
is sort of more perfect than it is, who will

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.319
say, we don't need to change the constitution,
uh, in any kind of significant way because

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:10.329
the constitution can be amended.

00:03:10.329 --> 00:03:12.909
Uh, it doesn't have to be replaced.

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:14.499
It's working as intended.

00:03:14.499 --> 00:03:18.599
The problem is that it's gotta be easier to
pass amendments if that's going to be your

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:19.599
argument.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:20.599
Right?

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:26.389
The constitution does provide for amending
it and it's been amended a couple dozen times.

00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:31.739
But in order to get an amendment through,
it needs to be proposed either by Congress

00:03:31.739 --> 00:03:36.819
with a two thirds majority vote in both the
house and the Senate, or by a constitutional

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:42.780
convention called four by two thirds of the
state legislatures in modern politics.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.340
This is almost impossible and you see it in
the results.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:53.049
The 27th amendment was passed in 1992 but
it related to the compensation of the members

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:54.379
of US Congress.

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:55.379
Okay?

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:56.840
It's about their own pay.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:03.700
If you go before the 27th amendment to the
26th it was in 1971 that's 48 years ago.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:10.980
That is a very long time for a system which
supposedly is ripe to be amended to make it

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.650
more relevant to modern day.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:21.560
The last 48 years have seen completely drastic
change culturally, technologically in terms

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.720
of legal precedent on so many different issues.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.450
So if your argument is going to be the constitution
is fine, as long as we keep up with amending

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.970
it, it's gotta be easier to amend it because
it's almost impossible, uh, in modern American

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:40.520
politics on anything other than how much do
senators and members of the House of Representatives

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:41.570
get paid?

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:42.570
Let me know what you think.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.920
But I do believe that the constitution is
an outdated document already

